A||||13||2birds||V-6 experts: exhaust gasket/cylinder head removal questions...||||1||
Z||000000||2birds||07-30-2001||11:43 PM||||I started to replace the leaking exhaust manifold gasket on my rear manifold today, only to discover that I had two of the six flange bolt heads missing.  The other four bolts came out easily, with the assistance of lots of PB blaster penetrant, and one of the broken bolts came out with a bit of coaxing (and more PB).  The 6th and final one, which is inconveniently the upper one by the alternator bracket, is being stubborn.  I'm leting it soak in penetrant overnight before I hit it with the vise grips again, buy I have a feeling I'll be pulling the head and taking it to a shop for removal.<BR>The heads have never been touched in 171,000 miles, but it is burning a bit of oil on startup, so I may just pull both heads and have them overhauled.  Having only messed with old Ford heads, I'm at a loss for what to have done to 2.8 heads.  Any suggestions on parts and specs from the engine experts out there? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||07-31-2001||08:14 AM||||Since you asked...  I wouldn't pull the head just yet.  Smoke on startup can be due to old, crumbly valve seals, which can be replaced without pulling the heads off.  If your compression readings are still good, I wouldn't pull the heads.<P>As for that last broken bolt, keep up the gentle persuasion and penetrating oil (Liquid Wrench or whatever), and hope for the best.  If the bolt breaks off flush, drill it out (step drill until there's just a shell of the original bolt left), then get the rest out using a left hand drill, left hand end mill (what I use), or a M8x1.25 tap.<P>I've gotten by without pulling my head out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> for a long time.  It's a lot less work and downtime (gasket scraping, cleaning, etc.). ||||reg||4
Z||000002||Raydar||07-31-2001||09:06 AM||||Just for grins, try smacking the end of the bolt (not the side) with a hammer. This will send a shock down the length of the bolt and, hopefully, break some of the rust loose. Don't hit it hard enough to deform the part that you're gonna grab with the vice-grips, though. Let it marinate in Liquid Wrench (or whatever) for as long as you can. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||07-31-2001||01:25 PM||||I've had good luck with Bee's Wax.  Heat up the bolt and push a wad of Bee's Wax against it.  The wax will melt and get sucked into the gap.||||reg||1
Z||000004||2birds||08-01-2001||10:30 PM||||Thanks for the input, as always.<BR>Well, two days of soaking in penetrant didn't help.  Yes, I whacked it quite a bit with a hammer to try and jar it loose.  Still no go.  The vise-grips were tearing the bolt apart, without budging a bit. So I tried heating the cylinder head face up with a propane torch, to see if a bit of heat would work.  I then put a pipe wrench on it for a better grip, and it sure gripped alright; So well it twisted the remaining exposed bit of bolt right off. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><BR>I'm going to pull the head now, maybe both just for giggles.  I pulled the upper plenum and vacuum tube snakepit off before it got dark last night, and called it quits.  It rained so much here today I said to hell with it and took the family to see Planet of the Apes.  I'll start again tomorrow.  I'm in no big hurry.||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||08-01-2001||11:35 PM||||welcome to Fieroland. Youve just started the number one repair PITA job that they all need. Wouldnt you like to get hold of the engineer who recommended those tiny, soft bolts for the exhaust.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>BTW whoever was responsible should be made to make the repairs to every one personally.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 08-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||LarryB||08-01-2001||11:40 PM||||The engineer probably spec'd the right part but got overridden by some beancounter looking to save 60 cents on every car.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||GT GT 87||08-02-2001||12:16 AM||||Don't forget to buy new head bolts. They are not reusable.||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||08-02-2001||06:20 AM||||true larry  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Carrolles||08-02-2001||08:36 AM||||I've read that many are using Saturn exhaust manifold studs/nuts or Stainless Steel studs in place of the stock Fiero exhaust manifold bolts.||||reg||1
Z||000010||StuGood||08-02-2001||12:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Carrolles:<BR><B>...many are using Saturn exhaust manifold studs/nuts or Stainless Steel studs in place of the stock Fiero exhaust manifold bolts.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I'm in the process of doing that now.  Part of me wants to put antiseize on the studs so that they can be removed if necessary.  Another part of me wants to put Loc*Tite on, so they won't vibrate loose.  Right now the "antiseize" lobby (in my head) has the upper hand.<P>Here's a link to recent thread on the subject of Saturn exhaust manifold attach hardware: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/012117.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/012117.html</A>   <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||08-02-2001||01:24 PM||||use either the Saturn kit or buy all stainless. also use anti seize like you figured, save you hassles down the road.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fast85GT||08-02-2001||06:09 PM||||Have fun with those front exhaust bolts.  I have remove mine twice for exhaust leaks.  45 minutes on my stomach on top of my engine...<P>------------------<BR>Jeremy H<BR>Black 1985 Pontiac Fiero GT||||reg||12
Z||000013||2birds||08-02-2001||09:58 PM||||This is definitely the biggest PITA job I've done in the 13+ years I've owned this car.<BR>Even worse than the clutch jobs.  At least I knew they would be a pain going into it.<P>I did the front manifold last March before I went out to sea. All the bolts came out easily, even the screwy ones they put in with the heat shield recall.  Two evenings of standing in the trunk and it was done.  I used all stainless steel hardware, courtesy of Lowe's Home Improvement, and plain ol' anti-sieze for the re-install.   So far, no leaks there.  <P>I bought the gasket set and head bolts tonight from Advance Auto.  Fel-pro set was $92 with tax, and they matched Discount Auto's price on the head bolts (also Fel-pro) for $21.  A local machine shop wants about $10 to pull the broken bolt out, and they will deck the heads for $30 each, and do a basic valve job for about the same, per head.  I may just put in a new set of stem seals, since it always smokes on startup.  I'll know more when I get them off.<BR>It only rained a bit today, but I just wasn't in the mood to work on it tonight. <P>Bad day at work and all that.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">||||reg||1
